unning a more  progressive candidacy for the times in  2008 actually worked to get him elected  hillary centrist in 2016  it also didn't work that's an important  lesson for the Democratic Party to learn  the centrism stuff Republican light  doesn't work but the progressive left  also needs to learn that when you lose  the raw vote by a million simply blaming  everything but some elements of either  the candidate or the way policies are  packaged or the framing or the messaging  or the surrogates or whatever right that  there has to be some evaluation that  isn't just look at the DNC we're not  going to win either as part of the  progressive left I say that so this is  very ugly if there is ever a realization  that social media support and crowd  sizes don't win you elections it should  be this primary and there is actually a  lot to learn here and it's not all bad  if you go back and check out my video  from a month or two ago gaming out the  different possible 20/20 outcomes this  is not all bad if it's taken as an  actual learning experience and applied  to Senate races in 2020 and to 2022 and  so on and so forth I want to hear from  you what do you believe are the lessons  from what's going on so far I'm on  Twitter at D Pakman let me know quick  break more after this
 

